  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry with cooler than normal temperatures today\nand tonight. \n\nSurface high pressure will be positioned the region today \nbringing dry conditions and mainly sunny skies. A cooler airmass\nwill be in place with 925mb temperatures several degrees cooler\nthan Monday. This will yield more \"Fall-like\" temperatures with\nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s and low 70s. Locations along\nthe immediate east coast may even be cooler in the mid 60s as a \nresult of the light easterly onshore flow. The high pressure \ncenter shifts offshore tonight shifting winds to the S then SW.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm temperatures return mid-week, \ncontinuing through Friday. A few showers possible sometime late \nThursday into early Friday, otherwise mainly dry. \n\nA strong upper level ridge/high remains positioned across the \nsoutheast CONUS mid-week, subtly building into the northeast mid-\nweek. An upper trough in northern Canada will hold off any stronger \nridging from building into the northeast, favoring a flatter/zonal \npattern overall. This should support warmer temperatures advecting \nin within the SW flow. Highs Wednesday/Thursday rise back into the \nmid and upper 70s with 80s in the valleys (seasonably warm for mid-\nSept). Surface high pressure should support mainly dry conditions \nWednesday and Thursday. \n\nEnsemble/deterministic guidance indicates potential for a weak \nshortwave trough to drop in from the northwest late Thursday into \nFriday. Ahead of this shortwave, a plume of anomalous moisture \nadvects in from the west on Thursday. Across ensemble guidance,\nthe PWATs run around 170-200 percent of normal or more directly\nput, PWATs 1.7-1.9\". The anomalous moisture is being signal \nacross NAEFS guidance as well. This building moisture will bring\na more humid day for Thursday. There is some spread across \nguidance with regards to the timing of the showers; however, \nthere is general agreement of the highest chances falling in the\nlate Thu afternoon to early Fri morning timeframe. That \ntimeframe should be able to be narrowed down as the details of \nthe pattern become more clear as we go through the week. Despite\nthe anomalous moisture, the weaker lift with this wave and \nlater timing has limited the potential for widespread heavy \nrain. Ensemble QPF remains below 0.50\" for most areas. We'll \nhave to keep an eye on some embedded heavier showers that could \nbring locally higher amounts. A drier airmass pushes in behind \nthe exiting shortwave Friday bringing dry conditions dominating \nby the afternoon. A cooler airmass sinks across the region by \nSaturday bring a return of Fall-like temperatures. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for a shift to an unsettled pattern\nwith chances or rain for second half of weekend through early \nnext week.\n\nEnsemble guidance hints at a transition to a more unsettled pattern \nfor the second half of the weekend and into early next week. A low \npressure system tracks eastward from the Great Lakes bringing \nincreased precipitation chances more likely in the Sun-Mon \ntimeframe. There indications of another wave or two bringing \nperiods of rain for early next week as well. Confidence in \nprecipitation chances and timing should increase as we get \ncloser.",
